title: Other heralds
pid: 1ca5facb-32d4-4414-8941-ebacd35d5158
body: |2-
  The position glyphs stand first among a headline's heralds, the ☮
  riding the glyph before it, as in =⊥☮B= or =ᗣ☮=.
  =ᗣ= marks a ghost (a node the worktree lacks, shown in the diff view
  with HEAD's text) and, in the diff view, what HEAD alone warrants
  (the atom =ghostWarrant= in =(view ...)=), such as a removed alias.
  =⊥= marks an unwarranted position (the atom
  =noWarrant= in =(view ...)=): the graph does not relate the node to
  its viewparent as the position implies -- content its parent does not
  contain, a folder member the folder's relation does not hold for, a
  chain node that does not contain the node above. =☮= marks a
  write-protected position: a node, a ghost, a folder, or a flag row.
  On a node, =B= rides the ☮ when the node's body exists but is
  omitted from the buffer. =⟳= marks a cycle.

  The forks folder reads =It forks these, or these fork it, or these fork or are forked by others here.=, with no ☮:
  it is editable. The labels of the three folders of what a note hides
  name the notes they relate by their ancestor letters (see [[id:b1ab14b7-2371-4ccf-a0dd-9c441b052aeb][Ancestor flags and floors]]),
  shown high (the style keys 'hiddenInForkPartnerFolder_hider',
  'hiddenOutsideOfForkPartnersFolder_hider',
  'hiddenByForkerFolder_hider' and
  'hiddenByForkerFolder_container' in
  'shared/herald-styles.json'). Under a member as such of a forks
  folder, a forkee or forker as such, the note whose folder holds it is
  the great-grandparent (the note, its forks folder, the member, the
  folder), and the label is the same in both positions, since the
  viewparent contains and the note hides: =☮ It contains these, but c
  hides them.= Under a forker as such that forks the note, the folder
  of what the forker, the viewparent, hides of the note's content reads
  =☮ a hides these, which c contains.= In the forks folder itself, the
  note is the grandparent: =b hides these, but no fork partner shown
  contains them.= (b's hides that no member of the forks folder,
  forkee, forker or indirect member, contains.) The hider and hidden folders read =☮ These hide
  it from their forks.= and =☮ It hides these from its forks.= The
  folder of what a member as such contains that the user keeps
  elsewhere, first under the member, names no ancestor: =☮ It contains
  these, and you keep them elsewhere.= (its members are born of the
  member's contains, =(contains in 2)=; 'docs/forks.org', "What you
  keep elsewhere").

  The bare atom =noClaim= (a child that does not affect its parent's
  membership on save) has no glyph of its own. Under an editable parent
  a child the graph warrants and that claims nothing is one the user
  marked =noClaim= to detach it: it shows =detach= until the next save
  takes it out of its parent's list. The client sees that from the
  parent, since the child's own metadata cannot say it. In a forks
  folder a forker-only or an indirect member claims nothing too; there a pending
  detachment is only a member whose =(rels (forks (in ...)))= names
  ancestor generation 2, which its grandparent still forks. =detach= is
  styled as a request, and listed with them above, but it is none:
  detaching writes only the =noClaim= atom, and the save reads that as
  it reads any claim. =C-c s t= ('skg-detach'; =:SkgDetach= in Neovim)
  makes one; nothing in a view the server draws is one, since every
  save consumes it. So =detach= means the next save removes that
  headline from its parent's list (in a forks folder, ends the fork),
  leaving the node itself and the headline in the buffer, shown =⊥=.
  To keep it in the list, =C-c s a= ('skg-attach'; =:SkgAttach=)
  cancels the detachment before the save.

  =⌂:LABEL= marks a node's home skgrepo boundary (the atom
  =repoBoundary= in =(view ...)=): a root, or a node whose home herald
  would say something other than what the nearest node above it says.
  A fork that records where it was forked from (its attribution,
  =(attribution NAME [unverified])= in =(view ...)=; =docs/forks.org=,
  "Attribution") reads =⌂:<LABEL/ORIGINAL>=: ORIGINAL is the label of
  the skgrepo it was forked from, followed by =?= when no loaded
  skgrepo is known to be the one recorded, its recorded name then
  shown. The comparison is of the whole herald, so a herald appears
  wherever the voice changes: under =⌂:<jeff/claude>=, a note of
  =jeff= with no attribution reads =⌂:jeff=, one forked from another
  skgrepo reads its own =⌂:<jeff/OTHER>=, and only a fork from the same
  =claude= says nothing; an attributed fork under a plain note of its
  own skgrepo reads =⌂:<jeff/claude>=. Folders between them do not
  count: the node above is the nearest node occurrence. LABEL is the skgrepo's skgrepo label,
  its =abbreviation= in the config or else its name, from the label
  table the server sends beside the herald rules. A pending move of the
  node's home skgrepo (=C-c s s=; =:SkgSetRepo=) marks the headline
  =repoBoundary= too, so until the save it reads =⌂:NEW=, NEW the
  label of the skgrepo it moves to; the save draws the boundaries it
  computes. A =~NAME= immediately
  before it reports a relationship stored at a more private relRepo than
  its default (never one stored in a foreign skgrepo, which has no
  privacy rank); =request:~NAME= is an explicit pending request to change
  that relationship's skgrepo on save. The home skgrepo belongs to the
  node; relRepo belongs to a relationship.

  Folder labels, =alias=, =ID= and the flag rows describe their view
  positions. The flags folder spells out =had ID before import=, =was
  overloaded during org-roam import=, and =no search matching= when true.
  =req:folder:...=, =req:flags=, =req:editable=,
  =req:write-protected=, =req:write-protected-subtree= and =req:fork=
  request a view.
  =delete=, =merge:...=, =request:strip attribution=, =request:no search matching=
  or =request:search matching=, and in the diff view =restore membership=
  and =restore node=, are save requests.

  An unknown viewnode, standing for an unknown graphnode, is labeled
  =Reference to unknown node.=. A
  restricted viewnode, standing where a record the skgrepo restriction
  shows names a node the skgrepo restriction keeps out, is labeled
  =restricted=, with its record's relRepo (=~NAME=) where that differs
  from the default, and reveals no node title or home skgrepo.
  =☮ ILLEGAL EDIT TO FOREIGN NODE=, in the message style (black on
  white), labels the illegal-edit folder (=illegalEditFolder=): a child of
  a foreign node holding a section of it in one of your skgrepos, which
  no save of yours could have made and which is no part of the node. Its
  headline carries that section's title, its body the section's body,
  and its children what the section contains; nothing in it is saved.
  See "Illegal edits" in =telescopes.org=.
  =something stood here= marks a tombstone: a headline with no title,
  left where a headline stood that can stand there for nothing, and
  holding that headline's children. The refresh leaves one where a
  node stood that the graph lacks and its viewparent's list does not
  name, such as one a save deleted (in the diff view, its ghost stands
  there instead); where a node the skgrepo restriction now keeps out
  stood and no record the skgrepo restriction shows names it; where a folder,
  a property member or a change viewnode stood in a place its kind
  cannot stand; where a change viewnode stood whose change is gone;
  and where a folder stood that was left holding nothing, unless the
  save reads its membership or it is the flags folder. A text search
  that excludes overPrivateText telescopes leaves one where the
  enrichment would have drawn such a node. A tombstone reveals no node
  title or skgrepo, nor which of these it was. The save discards text
  typed on its line, and no child of a tombstone claims membership. A
  tombstone that holds nothing is pruned before the buffer is shown.
